---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';

const pageTitle = "How to Sign an APK for Google Play Store Publishing";
const pageDescription = "Learn how to sign your SiteToApp APK: generate a keystore, sign the release, and upload to Google Play Console. Walkthrough with commands.";

const breadcrumb = {
  "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
  "itemListElement": [
    { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "Home", "item": "https://sitetoapp.dev/" },
    { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": "Guides", "item": "https://sitetoapp.dev/guides" },
    { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 3, "name": "How to Sign an APK for Google Play Store Publishing" }
  ]
};

const signApkSchema = { "@graph": [{
  "@type": "HowTo",
  "name": "How to sign an APK for Google Play Store publishing",
  "description": "Sign the Android APK generated by SiteToApp so Google Play accepts it: create an upload keystore, build the release APK, sign with apksigner, and verify the signature.",
  "step": [
    { "@type": "HowToStep", "name": "Generate an upload keystore", "text": "Create a keystore with keytool (Android Studio or the JDK) and store it safely — you will need the same key for every update." },
    { "@type": "HowToStep", "name": "Build the release APK", "text": "In your SiteToApp workspace ZIP, open the Android project and run ./gradlew assembleRelease (or use Android Studio's Generate Signed Bundle/APK wizard)." },
    { "@type": "HowToStep", "name": "Sign with apksigner", "text": "Sign the release APK: apksigner sign --ks <keystore> app-release.apk. Google Play requires a signature from your upload key." },
    { "@type": "HowToStep", "name": "Verify the signature", "text": "Run apksigner verify --print-certs app-release.apk and confirm it verifies successfully before uploading." },
    { "@type": "HowToStep", "name": "Upload to Google Play Console", "text": "Add the signed APK as a new release in Play Console, complete the store listing, and submit for review. Follow the full checklist in the Play Store publishing guide." }
  ]
}, breadcrumb]};
---

<Layout title={pageTitle} description={pageDescription} bodyClass="landing-page" structuredData={signApkSchema}>
  <main class="shell">
    <article class="content-panel">
      <h1>How to Sign an APK for Google Play Store Publishing</h1>
      <p>Every APK uploaded to the Google Play Store must be cryptographically signed with your developer upload key. This guide shows how to sign the APK that <a href="/android">SiteToApp</a> generates — from creating a keystore to verifying the signature — so your release is accepted by Play Console on the first attempt.</p>

      <h2>What signing does</h2>
      <p>An app signature proves the APK was built by you (or someone with your key). Google uses it to guarantee that updates to your app come from the same source. The debug APK you test with is <em>not</em> accepted for publishing — you need a release-signed build.</p>

      <h2>Step 1 — Generate an upload keystore</h2>
      <p>The easiest way is Android Studio: <strong>File → Project Structure → Signing &amp; Configs → Create new keystore</strong>. Or from the command line:</p>
      <pre>keytool -genkeypair -keystore upload-key.jks -alias upload -keyalg RSA -keysize 2048 -validity 10000</pre>
      <p>Choose a strong password and <strong>back the `.jks` file up in at least two secure places</strong>. If you lose it, you can still recover via Google Play's key recovery — but only if your identity is verified. Never commit it to a public repository: if a private key leaks, anyone can ship updates as you.</p>

      <h2>Step 2 — Build the release APK</h2>
      <p>Unzip the SiteToApp workspace ZIP. In the project root, run:</p>
      <pre>./gradlew assembleRelease</pre>
      <p>The unsigned release APK lands in <code>app/build/outputs/apk/release/</code>. (Android Studio's <em>Generate Signed Bundle / APK</em> wizard does steps 2–3 in one go if you prefer a GUI.)</p>

      <h2>Step 3 — Sign with apksigner</h2>
      <pre>apksigner sign --ks upload-key.jks --ks-key-alias upload --out app-signed.apk app-release.apk</pre>
      <p>Enter your keystore password when prompted. apksigner ships with the Android build tools (<code>~/Library/Android/sdk/build-tools/&lt;version&gt;/</code> on macOS, <code>/opt/android-sdk/build-tools</code> on Linux).</p>

      <h2>Step 4 — Verify the signature</h2>
      <pre>apksigner verify --print-certs app-signed.apk</pre>
      <p>If verification passes, the output shows your certificate SHA-256 digest. Install the signed APK on a device as a final sanity check, then upload it.</p>

      <h2>Step 5 — Upload to Play Console</h2>
      <p>Head to <strong>Production → Create new release → Upload</strong> and add <code>app-signed.apk</code>. Then finish the store listing and submit — our <a href="/guides/publish-to-play-store">complete Play Store publishing guide</a> covers every screen of that process.</p>

      <h2>Sign once, update forever</h2>
      <p>Keep the same upload key for every version of your app. Future releases just run the same three commands with a bumped <code>versionCode</code> in <code>build.gradle</code> — and because the app loads your live website, content updates never need a recompile at all.</p>

      <p>Not built your app yet? <a href="/android"><strong>Generate the wrapper project</strong></a>, or compare the desktop options in our <a href="/guides/website-to-desktop-windows">website-to-Windows-desktop guide</a>.</p>
    </article>
  </main>
</Layout>
